/* data/learn-c/chem-11-01.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/chem-11-01.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-11-01"] = {"1.1":{"s":"Chemistry is the study of matter: what it is made of, how it behaves, and how it changes. Everything around us, from food and medicines to fuels and fabrics, is made of chemical substances. This chapter starts the course with the basic ideas that all later chapters use: the nature and properties of matter, the laws of chemical combination, atoms and molecules, and the mole.","x":["Chemistry is the study of matter: what it is made of, how it behaves and how it changes into other kinds of matter. Food, medicines, fuels, fabrics, soap and fertilisers are all chemical substances, or are made by chemical changes.","This first chapter gives the ideas that every later chapter uses. They are: what matter is and how it is classified, the properties of matter and how we measure them, the laws that describe how substances combine, atoms and molecules and their masses, and the mole, which links the mass of a sample to the number of particles in it.","Study tip: the chapter is the base for all calculations in chemistry. Practise the mole calculations until they are quick, because they return in every chapter of Class 11 and Class 12."],"e":"Baking a cake is a chemical change. The baking powder gives carbon dioxide gas, which makes the cake rise. The change cannot be reversed, which is a mark of a chemical change."},"1.2":{"s":"Matter is anything that has mass and occupies space. It exists in three physical states, solid, liquid and gas. Pure substances are elements and compounds. An element has one kind of atom, and a compound has atoms of different elements joined in a fixed ratio. Mixtures are formed by physically mixing substances. A homogeneous mixture has the same composition throughout; a heterogeneous mixture does not.","w":["A compound has a fixed composition and different properties from its elements; a mixture has a variable composition."],"x":["Matter is anything that has mass and takes up space. It exists in three physical states. A solid has a fixed shape and volume. A liquid has a fixed volume but takes the shape of its container. A gas has neither a fixed shape nor a fixed volume.","Pure substances are of two kinds. An element has only one kind of atom (iron, oxygen). A compound has atoms of two or more elements joined in a fixed ratio (water, H₂O, has 2 hydrogen atoms for 1 oxygen atom). The properties of a compound are different from those of its elements.","A mixture is formed by mixing two or more substances physically, in any proportion. A homogeneous mixture has the same composition throughout, like salt water or air. A heterogeneous mixture does not, like sand in water. The parts of a mixture can be separated by physical methods such as filtering, evaporation or distillation. A compound can be broken up only by a chemical change.","Common mistake: thinking that air is a compound. Air is a homogeneous mixture of gases, with variable composition."],"e":"Classify: gold (element), sugar (compound), sugar dissolved in water (homogeneous mixture), mud in water (heterogeneous mixture)."},"1.3":{"s":"The properties of matter are physical or chemical. Physical properties, such as colour, melting point, boiling point and density, can be measured or seen without changing what the substance is. Chemical properties show up only when the substance reacts and changes into something else. Measurements are written with a number and a unit, and the SI system is the standard set of units used all over the world.","w":["Burning, rusting and souring are chemical changes; melting and boiling are physical changes.","A measurement is incomplete without its unit."],"x":["Properties of matter are physical or chemical. Physical properties, such as colour, odour, melting point, boiling point and density, can be observed or measured without changing the identity of the substance. Chemical properties, such as the ability to burn or to rust, show only when the substance reacts and turns into a new substance.","A physical change alters only the form or state of a substance, like melting ice or boiling water. A chemical change makes new substances, like burning wood, rusting of iron or souring of milk. Signs of a chemical change are a new colour, a gas, a precipitate, or a change in temperature.","A measurement has two parts, a number and a unit. The SI system is the international standard. Its seven base quantities are length (metre), mass (kilogram), time (second), electric current (ampere), temperature (kelvin), amount of substance (mole) and luminous intensity (candela). Prefixes like kilo, milli and micro change the size of a unit by powers of ten.","Common mistakes: leaving out the unit, and mixing units (for example grams with kilograms) inside one calculation. Convert first, then calculate."],"e":"Convert 250 mL to litres and then to dm³. 250 mL = 0.250 L, and 1 L = 1 dm³, so the volume is 0.250 dm³. The density of a liquid of mass 200 g in this volume is 200/250 = 0.8 g/mL."},"1.4":{"s":"Chemical combination follows a few laws. The law of conservation of mass: matter is neither created nor destroyed in a reaction. The law of definite proportions: a pure compound always has the same elements in the same ratio by mass. The law of multiple proportions: when two elements form more than one compound, the masses of one that combine with a fixed mass of the other are in a simple whole-number ratio. Gay-Lussac's law deals with gas volumes.","w":["Match each law to its example: water always has 1 part hydrogen to 8 parts oxygen by mass (definite proportions); CO and CO₂ (multiple proportions).","Mass is conserved in a reaction, but the number of molecules need not be."],"x":["Law of conservation of mass: in a chemical reaction, the total mass of the reactants equals the total mass of the products. Matter is neither created nor destroyed. This is why a chemical equation must be balanced.","Law of definite proportions: a pure compound always contains the same elements in the same proportion by mass, whatever its source. Water always has 1 part of hydrogen to 8 parts of oxygen by mass.","Law of multiple proportions: two elements can make several different compounds. Fix the mass of the second element. Then the masses of the first element in these compounds are in a small whole-number ratio to each other. In carbon monoxide and carbon dioxide, 12 g of carbon combines with 16 g and 32 g of oxygen. The ratio is 1 : 2.","Gay-Lussac's law of combining volumes: gases react in volumes that are in simple whole-number ratios, if measured at the same temperature and pressure. For example, hydrogen and oxygen combine in the volume ratio 2 : 1, and the steam formed has the same volume as the hydrogen used.","Common mistake: believing that the number of molecules is conserved. Only mass and the number of atoms of each element are conserved."],"e":"Two oxides of nitrogen contain 28 g of nitrogen with 16 g and 32 g of oxygen. The oxygen masses for a fixed 28 g of nitrogen are in the ratio 16 : 32 = 1 : 2, a simple ratio. This supports the law of multiple proportions."},"1.5":{"s":"Avogadro's law: two gas samples of the same volume, measured at one temperature and one pressure, contain the same number of molecules, whatever the gas. It explains Gay-Lussac's observation that gases react in simple volume ratios. The law also led to the idea that gas molecules such as hydrogen and oxygen are made of two atoms. It is the base of the molar volume of a gas.","w":["Avogadro's law needs the same temperature and pressure; it is about the number of molecules, not the mass."],"x":["Avogadro's law: take two gas samples with the same volume, at one temperature and one pressure. They hold the same number of molecules, whichever gases they are. The law counts molecules, not mass and not kind.","The law explains Gay-Lussac's observations. Since the volumes are in simple ratios, the numbers of molecules that react are in the same ratios. It also shows that molecules of gases like hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen are diatomic. For example, one volume of hydrogen and one volume of chlorine give two volumes of hydrogen chloride. One molecule of each gives two molecules of the product, so each of the elements must be made of two atoms.","From this law comes the idea of molar volume. One mole of any gas occupies the same volume at the same temperature and pressure. At 0 °C and 1 atm, it is 22.4 L, as the next topics show.","Common mistake: using the law for gases at different temperatures or pressures. The condition 'same temperature and pressure' is essential."],"e":"Two litres of hydrogen and two litres of oxygen, both at 300 K and 1 atm, contain the same number of molecules. But the oxygen has 16 times the mass of the hydrogen, as its molar mass is 32 and that of hydrogen is 2."},"1.6":{"s":"Dalton's atomic theory, put forward in 1808, says that matter is made of indivisible atoms. All atoms of an element are alike, and they differ from the atoms of other elements. Atoms combine in small whole-number ratios to make compounds, and a reaction only rearranges atoms; they are neither created nor destroyed. The theory explains the laws of chemical combination. Later work showed that atoms are divisible and that isotopes exist.","w":["Parts of Dalton's theory were later changed: atoms are divisible, and atoms of one element can have different masses (isotopes)."],"x":["Dalton's atomic theory (1808) said that matter is made of very small particles called atoms, which cannot be divided, created or destroyed. All the atoms of one element are identical in mass and properties, and are different from the atoms of other elements.","Atoms of different elements combine in small whole-number ratios to form compounds. A chemical reaction is only a rearrangement of atoms: the atoms are separated and joined in new ways.","The theory explains the laws of chemical combination. Conservation of mass follows because atoms are not created or destroyed. Definite proportions follow because a compound always has the same numbers of atoms of each element. Multiple proportions follow because atoms combine in whole-number ratios.","Limits of the theory: atoms are divisible (they have electrons, protons and neutrons), and atoms of the same element can differ in mass (isotopes). The theory is still the basis of chemical calculations."],"e":"Why is the mass of water formed from 4 g of hydrogen and 32 g of oxygen equal to 36 g? Atoms are only rearranged, not lost. So the mass of the product equals the total mass of the reactants."},"1.7":{"s":"The mass of an atom is tiny, so it is given in atomic mass units. One atomic mass unit, u, is one twelfth of the mass of a carbon-12 atom. The atomic mass of an element is its mass in u, averaged over its natural isotopes. The molecular mass of a molecule is the sum of the atomic masses of the atoms in it. For ionic compounds the term formula mass is used.","w":["Atomic mass is an average over the isotopes, so it is usually not a whole number."],"x":["Atoms are so small that their masses are written on a special scale. One atomic mass unit (u) is one twelfth of the mass of one atom of carbon-12. It is about 1.66 × 10⁻²⁷ kg. The mass of a carbon-12 atom is exactly 12 u.","The atomic mass of an element is the average mass of its atoms, in u, calculated from the masses of its isotopes and their abundances in nature. For chlorine, with 35Cl (about 75 percent) and 37Cl (about 25 percent), the average is about 35.5 u. This is why atomic masses are usually not whole numbers.","The molecular mass of a molecule is the sum of the atomic masses of all the atoms in it. For H₂O it is 2(1) + 16 = 18 u. For ionic compounds, which have no separate molecules, the sum for one formula unit is called the formula mass: for NaCl it is 23 + 35.5 = 58.5 u.","Common mistake: forgetting to multiply the atomic mass by the number of atoms in the formula, for example in H₂SO₄ (2 × 1 + 32 + 4 × 16 = 98 u)."],"e":"Find the average atomic mass of an element with two isotopes: 10 u with 20 percent abundance and 11 u with 80 percent. The average is 0.20 × 10 + 0.80 × 11 = 2 + 8.8 = 10.8 u."},"1.8":{"s":"A mole is the amount of a substance that has as many particles as there are atoms in 12 g of carbon-12. This number is Avogadro's constant, about 6.022 × 10²³ per mole. The molar mass of a substance is the mass of one mole in grams; it has the same number as the atomic or molecular mass in u. These two facts link the mass of a sample to the number of moles and to the number of particles.","f":[{"x":"N_A = 6.022*10^23 mol^-1","n":"Avogadro's constant: number of particles in one mole.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 1.8, p.9"},{"x":"n = mass/molar mass","n":"Number of moles n; the mass in grams and the molar mass in g/mol.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 1.8, p.9"},{"x":"N = n*N_A","n":"Number of particles N (atoms, molecules or ions) in n moles.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 1.8, p.9"}],"w":["Molar mass is in g/mol, atomic or molecular mass is in u: the numbers are equal but the units differ.","Mole is a count of particles: say whether you mean moles of atoms or moles of molecules (1 mol O₂ has 2 mol O atoms)."],"x":["A mole is the amount of a substance that contains as many elementary particles (atoms, molecules, ions) as there are atoms in 12 g of carbon-12. This number is called Avogadro's constant, N_A = 6.022 × 10²³ per mole.","The molar mass of a substance is the mass of one mole of it, in g/mol. It is numerically equal to the atomic or molecular mass in u. For water, the molecular mass is 18 u, so the molar mass is 18 g/mol.","Three formulas link everything: the number of moles n = mass/molar mass; the number of particles N = n × N_A; and so mass → moles → particles. To go from mass to the number of atoms, first find the moles and then multiply by N_A, and by the number of atoms per molecule if needed.","Common mistakes: confusing the moles of molecules with the moles of atoms (one mole of O₂ has two moles of oxygen atoms), and mixing the units u and g/mol. The numbers are the same, but u is for one particle, and g/mol is for one mole."],"e":"How many molecules are in 9 g of water? n = 9/18 = 0.5 mol. N = 0.5 × 6.022 × 10²³ = 3.011 × 10²³ molecules. Each has 3 atoms, so there are 9.033 × 10²³ atoms."},"1.9":{"s":"Gases are easier to measure by volume than by mass. By Avogadro's law, one mole of any gas takes the same volume under the same conditions. At standard temperature and pressure, this molar volume is 22.4 dm³ per mole. So the number of moles of a gas equals its volume at STP divided by the molar volume. Multiplying by Avogadro's constant then gives the number of molecules.","f":[{"x":"n = V/22.4 dm^3 mol^-1","n":"Moles of a gas from its volume V at STP (0 degree Celsius, 1 atm).","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XI, 1.9, p.10"}],"w":["22.4 dm³ per mole is for the old STP (0 °C and 1 atm). With 1 bar the molar volume is 22.71 L.","Molar volume applies to gases only, not to liquids or solids."],"x":["Gases are easier to measure by volume than by mass. By Avogadro's law, one mole of any gas occupies the same volume at the same temperature and pressure. This is the molar volume.","At standard temperature and pressure in the older definition (0 °C and 1 atm), the molar volume of an ideal gas is 22.4 L, which is 22.4 dm³. With the newer standard (273.15 K and 1 bar) it is 22.7 L. State which one a problem uses. Molar volume applies only to gases.","Formulas: n = V/22.4 for V in litres at 0 °C and 1 atm. The number of molecules is N = n × 6.022 × 10²³. The mass is m = n × molar mass. For example, 11.2 L of oxygen at 0 °C and 1 atm is 0.5 mol, with a mass of 0.5 × 32 = 16 g.","For gases at other temperatures and pressures, use pV = nRT. Common mistake: using 22.4 L/mol for a gas at room temperature. At 25 °C and 1 atm, the molar volume is about 24.5 L."],"e":"What volume does 2.2 g of CO₂ occupy at 0 °C and 1 atm? n = 2.2/44 = 0.05 mol. V = 0.05 × 22.4 = 1.12 L."}};
